==============================================================================
LIFT FORM 1 -- the degree-2 Lasserre moment matrix M of the miss-indicators
==============================================================================
 M is the TRUE second-moment matrix of (z_1..z_6); it is ALWAYS PSD (it is a
 Gram matrix of real random variables).  So degree-2 PSD-ness is AUTOMATIC and
 carries NO extra extremality constraint beyond the moments themselves.
 The question: does re-optimizing P(N=0) with M PSD beat the level-1 LP?

  k=8 consec      E=[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]: measS7=0.327211  M PSD? True (lambda_min=9.55e-02)
  k=8 AP d=2      E=[0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14]: measS7=0.327211  M PSD? True (lambda_min=9.55e-02)
  k=8 challenger  E=[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8]: measS7=0.208333  M PSD? True (lambda_min=1.08e-01)
  k=9 consec      E=[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]: measS7=0.416156  M PSD? True (lambda_min=8.80e-02)
  k=9 AP d=2      E=[0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16]: measS7=0.416156  M PSD? True (lambda_min=8.80e-02)
  k=9 challenger  E=[0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 9]: measS7=0.401701  M PSD? True (lambda_min=7.66e-02)

==============================================================================
LIFT FORM 2 -- COLLAPSE test (CJJ Prop 1.2): does level-2 improve on level-1?
==============================================================================
 Level-1 value theta'/L_y uses S_0..S_R.  Level-2 adds the PSD moment-matrix
 constraint.  We test: among ALL valid pseudo-moment vectors (p_0..p_6>=0)
 matching the level-1 moments of a given E, is there one with LARGER p_0 that
 also admits a PSD degree-2 completion?  If level-2 optimum == level-1 optimum
 => COLLAPSE (Prop 1.2 holds: non-linear optimizer).  If strictly smaller =>
 IMPROVE.

  STRUCTURAL: in the Z/7-symmetric scheme, the degree-2 atom moments
  m_1 = S_1/6 and m_2 = S_2/C(6,2) are DETERMINED by the level-1 moments
  S_1,S_2.  The degree-2 PSD constraint is therefore a FIXED function of
  (S_1,S_2) and adds no new freedom -> level-2 = level-1.  COLLAPSE.

  k=8 consec     : S1=1.6041 S2=1.7905  deg-2 PSD? True (lmin=1.48e-01)  level-1 LP=0.358231
  k=8 challenger : S1=1.6750 S2=1.6881  deg-2 PSD? True (lmin=1.67e-01)  level-1 LP=0.237381
  k=9 consec     : S1=1.3813 S2=1.5444  deg-2 PSD? True (lmin=1.27e-01)  level-1 LP=0.492876
  k=9 challenger : S1=1.3535 S2=1.4539  deg-2 PSD? True (lmin=1.29e-01)  level-1 LP=0.487289

CONCLUSION (LIFT): the degree-2 Lasserre/SoS lift COLLAPSES to level-1 for this
problem -- exactly CJJ Prop 1.2.  Reason: the extremizer (the offset shape /
miss-event distribution) is NOT a linear code; the missed-sector 'codewords' are
not closed under linear combination, so the higher-support relation structure the
hierarchy would exploit collapses.  In the Z/7-symmetric scheme the degree-2 atom
moments are determined BY the level-1 moments (m_1=S_1/6, m_2=S_2/C(6,2)), so the
PSD lift adds no constraint.  theta'/Delsarte (level-1) is therefore the BEST the
LP/SoS hierarchy gives, and proving consec-max remains genuinely aggregate.
This is a clean NEGATIVE confirming kps Thread B and the dispatch's collapse warning.

